# Normalize line endings in the repository.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites checked-out
# files to CRLF. That silently breaks anything whose CONTENT is load-bearing:
# the seeded agent fixtures in packages/ggcoder are content-hashed, and their
# SHA-256 changed on Windows purely because of the line endings, failing
# config.test.ts on every Windows CI run.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores text as LF and checks it out as LF everywhere, so a
# Windows contributor gets byte-identical files to CI and macOS. Editors have
# handled LF for many years; the actual risk is the reverse.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary — never touch these, and don't diff them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.node binary

# Windows-only scripts that a native shell must be able to parse.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
